In article <999@kosman.UUCP> kevin@kosman.UUCP (Root) writes:
>In article <281@spcc386.UUCP> stefan@spcc386.UUCP (Stefan Posthuma) writes:
>>
>>How about a new tool, a whetstone. You could apply it to swords and other
>>blades, to add a few pluses. It would be just like a scroll of enchant
>>weapon, but this time there is noting magical about it. After a few uses
>>it will crumble to pieces or something.
>
>I like the idea of a whetstone, and I think it should last a long time.
>This mimics the way real 'stones work.  However, I think it should be rare,
>and not able to create more than +1.  Thus, you would use it to repair
>damage.  Real swords get nicks and burrs when used, and they require
>maintenance on the part of the user.

My experience with FRP games has been with the interpretation that making 
alterations to magic items spoils their dweomer completely. Therefore I find
myself resisting the idea of "adding" plusses to a magic weapon in such a
manner. The same FRP games, however, have rust- and nick/burr-resistance
included in the dweomer, while Nethack has not. Therefore, I am in favor of
allowing "sharpness" improvement even to magic weapons, although I agree that
the improvement should be limited to +1, and this only to damage, not to 
hitting probabilities. 

To do this correctly, I think sharpness would be tracked separately from 
weapon plus, with a variable representing how many times the weapon has struck 
another object or creature. (If one wanted to be *really* fancy, one could 
adjust this based on the hardness of the creature or object struck...). At a 
certain "high-water mark", the weapon is "dull", and would be identified as such
(e.g. "a dull +0 long sword"), at which time a whetstone could be used to 
restore it. Any "special" physical damage, such as acid or rust, would affect 
sharpness first, *then* eat away the weapon to such a degree that it could 
*never* be sharpened again. 

Starting weapons would always be "sharp", bones-file weapons would inherit 
their sharpness, weapons taken from monsters would have randomly-generated
sharpnesses, and weapons found just laying in the dungeon would always be 
"dull". 

Exceptions could be made, of course, for things like artifacts, which might
*always* have a certain sharpness when acquired and/or never change.

Concerns: a) is this *really* worth the extra coding? (I would be willing to 
take this on, if people really think it's useful), b) will this make weapon
names ridiculous (e.g. "a dull +3 long sword named Excalibur (weapon in 
hand)")?, c) does this require players to keep track of too much?, d) does 
this give certain classes (i.e. the ones with edged weapons) an unfair 
advantage or disadvantage?, e) does this "dilute" the tool category even 
further?

P.S. Maybe "gray stones" could be usable as whetstones.

P.P.S. Pardon my ignorance, but is water necessary for this kind of sharpening?
       *Any* kind of liquid? Potions? Spit?
